1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:03,588
So continuing on the previous
boulder tips video,

2
00:00:03,588 --> 00:00:06,384
I'm going to highlight a few
additional boulder at tips.

3
00:00:06,384 --> 00:00:09,586
That kind of come up as sort of
a frequently asked questions for

4
00:00:09,586 --> 00:00:11,261
the first boulder assignment.

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,069
So we're going to talk about some general
build debug strategies specifically for

6
00:00:16,069 --> 00:00:17,551
assignment for boulder component.

7
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:22,845
So first of all you can get some really
confusing things if you check binaries

8
00:00:22,845 --> 00:00:23,629
into your repo.

9
00:00:23,629 --> 00:00:28,658
And the reason this can happen is if
you native compile an application and

10
00:00:28,658 --> 00:00:32,140
then you check in the binary object file.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:36,136
Boulder is going to think that it doesn't
have anything to do to rebuild that file,

12
00:00:36,136 --> 00:00:38,441
it's going to see that
the object file is there.

13
00:00:38,441 --> 00:00:41,783
It's going to think that there's
no compile step that's needed and

14
00:00:41,783 --> 00:00:43,464
it's going to try to use your file.

15
00:00:43,464 --> 00:00:46,828
When it tries to use your file it's
going to be the wrong architecture and

16
00:00:46,828 --> 00:00:49,640
so it's not going to work
on the Arm 64 target.

17
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:53,995
So you need to make sure that you've got
your get ignored set up to keep any up,

18
00:00:53,995 --> 00:00:56,940
executable or
object files out of your repo.

19
00:00:56,940 --> 00:01:00,880
And this should be set up by default for
you on the base repo.

20
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:03,511
But it just be a matter of
if you rename something or

21
00:01:03,511 --> 00:01:06,344
if you didn't follow this pattern for
object files.

22
00:01:06,344 --> 00:01:09,961
So something to keep in mind, make sure
that that get ignores set correctly.

23
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:15,342
Also don't hard code any references
to gcc in your make file,

24
00:01:15,342 --> 00:01:17,510
use CC as a compiler variable.

25
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:22,020
And this allows if you use these
compiler variables shown here for

26
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:23,430
your compile step.

27
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:28,262
This allows builder to define
them the way it needs to do to

28
00:01:28,262 --> 00:01:30,688
do its cross compile setup.

29
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:34,295
And finally pay attention to
the configuration changes and

30
00:01:34,295 --> 00:01:36,260
the output of save config.sh.

31
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:40,925
So you're going to run save config to save
off the configurations you've got for

32
00:01:40,925 --> 00:01:41,709
buildroot.

33
00:01:41,709 --> 00:01:46,354
And make sure that when you do that step,
you save that off into your repo.

34
00:01:46,354 --> 00:01:51,513
If you forget to do that,
then when you check out to a new repo or

35
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:56,089
maybe when you deploy and
run your automated testing.

36
00:01:56,089 --> 00:01:59,372
You're not going to have
the same configuration that

37
00:01:59,372 --> 00:02:02,586
you used when you originally
checked in the code.

38
00:02:02,586 --> 00:02:06,955
Some general debug strategies
that work well for

39
00:02:06,955 --> 00:02:11,121
buildroot, what I like
to do is try to create.

40
00:02:11,121 --> 00:02:17,032
Especially in cases where you aren't
getting the output that you expected.

41
00:02:17,032 --> 00:02:19,495
It doesn't appear that
the changes you're making or

42
00:02:19,495 --> 00:02:22,840
making any difference in the output,
try to just make an error.

43
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:26,384
So use something that's outside of
makes in tax, for instance, for

44
00:02:26,384 --> 00:02:30,640
a dot make file or just type some garbage
on the line and I can figure that in.

45
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:34,077
And what this will tell you is whether
you've got the configuration set

46
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:37,530
up correctly and buildroot to actually
read these files to begin with.

47
00:02:37,530 --> 00:02:41,326
And a lot of times some of the problem
is that the file that you think is being

48
00:02:41,326 --> 00:02:44,161
included actually isn't
even being included at all.

49
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,821
Also pay attention to
trying incremental builds,

50
00:02:48,821 --> 00:02:52,442
there's some content that we
discussed in lecture about this.

51
00:02:52,442 --> 00:02:57,040
And there's some information in
the buildroot help files about it.

52
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:00,512
But if you do make command from
the builder at subdirectory and

53
00:03:00,512 --> 00:03:02,463
you use this override sourcedir.

54
00:03:02,463 --> 00:03:10,080
You can specify an arbitrary path and
rebuild directly from a local directory.

55
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,956
So this saves the need to if you're
doing a lot of trouble shooting or

56
00:03:13,956 --> 00:03:17,370
incremental steps to try to
troubleshoot some problem.

57
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:21,780
It saves you the need from having to
push your AESD assignments repo up.

58
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:26,202
Change the git hash and rebuild from
buildroot and that whole process just gets

59
00:03:26,202 --> 00:03:29,440
time consuming if you need
to do a lot of iterations.

60
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:32,907
So look at how to use the override
sourcedir variable and

61
00:03:32,907 --> 00:03:35,507
use this AESD assignments rebuild task.

62
00:03:35,507 --> 00:03:42,240
To rebuild just your AESD
assignments module or yeah module.

63
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:45,672
And also one thing to keep
in mind with buildroot,

64
00:03:45,672 --> 00:03:48,550
try to use clean build as the last result.

65
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:53,398
Clean build while it's faster than
yak to which we'll talk about

66
00:03:53,398 --> 00:03:56,137
in the next course in this sequence.

67
00:03:56,137 --> 00:04:00,006
It still takes quite a bit of time and
so it's not the kind of thing that

68
00:04:00,006 --> 00:04:03,901
you want to do if you're used to
just compiling a native application.

69
00:04:03,901 --> 00:04:06,283
Maybe you just think I'll
just run make clean and

70
00:04:06,283 --> 00:04:08,160
then I'll rebuild the application.

71
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:11,671
But if you do a clean step on buildroot
it's going to take at least a half hour

72
00:04:11,671 --> 00:04:14,281
depending on the amount of
your system to rebuild it.

73
00:04:14,281 --> 00:04:18,573
So it's not a normal debug step that
you'd want to take just any time,

74
00:04:18,573 --> 00:04:21,040
something isn't working.

75
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:27,101
A few other builders debug strategies,
this is really an optimization thing.

76
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:30,412
You can use DL DIR to
avoid download steps and

77
00:04:30,412 --> 00:04:34,280
downloads can take quite
a bit of time to complete.

78
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:35,561
So if you use your DL DIR,

79
00:04:35,561 --> 00:04:38,491
you can keep those out of your
builder output directory.

80
00:04:38,491 --> 00:04:40,682
And that way if you do a clean,

81
00:04:40,682 --> 00:04:45,640
you won't have to re download
all of the package source code.

82
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:50,788
One other kind of useful thing
with this if you've got a friend

83
00:04:50,788 --> 00:04:57,050
who is a local maybe you could share
a flash key with or something like that.

84
00:04:57,050 --> 00:05:01,121
You can put your DL DIR on a flash key,
share that with your friend and

85
00:05:01,121 --> 00:05:05,837
then they don't have to wait for all the
files to download from the internet for

86
00:05:05,837 --> 00:05:06,921
that directory.

87
00:05:06,921 --> 00:05:11,273
So there's some notes about this
in the link in the assignment, so

88
00:05:11,273 --> 00:05:12,602
take a look at that.

89
00:05:12,602 --> 00:05:16,725
And also do really close inspection
when you're having problems,

90
00:05:16,725 --> 00:05:19,930
whatever you think is
associated with the problem.

91
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:22,555
Look really closely at
every single character and

92
00:05:22,555 --> 00:05:24,248
this includes spaces and tabs.

93
00:05:24,248 --> 00:05:28,360
Make sure that you've specified the
variable exactly the way you want it to.

94
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:31,090
You didn't include any
extra things like colons or

95
00:05:31,090 --> 00:05:33,900
something like that at
the end of the variable name.

96
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:38,799
It's very finicky sometimes about getting
things printed out exactly right, so

97
00:05:38,799 --> 00:05:40,380
what works well for this?

98
00:05:40,380 --> 00:05:44,200
I've got some working examples that I've
shared that you can use as a reference.

99
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:47,153
And a lot of times if you just
put those side by side and

100
00:05:47,153 --> 00:05:49,177
look just character by character.

101
00:05:49,177 --> 00:05:51,771
Sometimes you can see a difference
that will explain the problem that

102
00:05:51,771 --> 00:05:52,451
you're seeing.

103
00:05:53,610 --> 00:05:56,968
One other thing to think about is think
of other ways that you could test out

104
00:05:56,968 --> 00:05:59,540
whatever implementation you're working on.

105
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:03,542
And so one of the things I've tried to do
with these assignments is make it possible

106
00:06:03,542 --> 00:06:06,630
for you to run the assignment,
both natively and in embedded.

107
00:06:06,630 --> 00:06:11,190
And so it's probably going to be a lot
faster to do iteration and debug natively.

108
00:06:11,190 --> 00:06:16,239
So think about how you could reproduce
the problem on your native compile.

109
00:06:16,239 --> 00:06:23,279
And maybe troubleshooting in debug there
as an alternative to debugging inside QMU.

110
00:06:23,279 --> 00:06:29,491
Few other suggestions for buildroot debug,
don't wait until the last minute to start.

111
00:06:29,491 --> 00:06:31,188
So the build especially for

112
00:06:31,188 --> 00:06:36,240
buildroot can take quite a bit of time
depending on how fast your system is.

113
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:41,494
So think about maybe before you get
everything implemented with your program.

114
00:06:41,494 --> 00:06:44,303
Kicking off a buildroot build,
at least getting that started,

115
00:06:44,303 --> 00:06:46,601
getting all the downloads done,
start that early.

116
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:49,361
Don't wait till the very
end to start that part and

117
00:06:49,361 --> 00:06:52,129
think about all the things
you can do in parallel.

118
00:06:52,129 --> 00:06:55,558
So while you're waiting for downloads or
builds to happen, I think if there's

119
00:06:55,558 --> 00:06:58,745
something that you can do to be working
on the next part of the assignment.

120
00:06:58,745 --> 00:07:03,040
And have that ready and maybe you can even
set up a new branch and start working on

121
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:07,940
that before you're ready to actually
pull that into your assignment.

122
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:12,778
And one of the things I like to do
when having issues with any problem.

123
00:07:12,778 --> 00:07:17,267
But something like this is maybe just step
away from the problem for a few hours and

124
00:07:17,267 --> 00:07:18,201
come back to it.

125
00:07:18,201 --> 00:07:21,818
I always find that I can, if I take
some time away from it a lot of times

126
00:07:21,818 --> 00:07:25,340
when I come back to it,
I'll think about it slightly different.

127
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:28,803
This is another case where it's important
to start early to have the flexibility to

128
00:07:28,803 --> 00:07:29,751
do that kind of thing.